    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d138">

                <pb facs="0487" n="1301" xml:id="pg1301"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Presidential Correspondence files, lot 66 D 204,
                    “Churchill Correspondence”</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President
                        Eisenhower</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d138-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">personal and private</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-12-16">December 16, 1953</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I have to tell the House of Commons tomorrow about our meeting<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d138-f2"/> and I send you a draft of what I have prepared on atomic
                    affairs. I hope that you only need to send me an O.K. as time is so very short
                    before I speak at 3:30 p.m. G. M. T. tomorrow Thursday.</p>
                <p>I have included nothing of what I shall say about our talks and interchanges upon
                    your U.N. speech as I am giving full support to your inspiring lead and trying
                    to persuade the bear to stop growling. I shall make it clear that I no more
                    write your speeches than you write mine and I expect to stave off questions
                    about the untruthful press, rumours of which there have certainly been no
                    lack.</p>
                <p>I shall defend Foster for speaking frankly to the French in Paris about
                        EDC.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d138-f3"/> Anthony and I both believe the
                    secondary reaction may be favourable.</p>
                <p>Kindest regards,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Winston</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d138-a1.xml" xpointer="d138-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d138-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d138-f1-content">
                <p>A covering “Memorandum for the Acting Secretary of State” from Presidential
                    Secretary Ann C. Whitman reads: “Attached is copy of a message the President
                    just received from the Prime Minister.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d138-f2" n="2" xml:id="d138-f2-content">
                <p>Prime Minister Churchill’s remarks to the House of Commons on his meeting with
                    President Eisenhower and Premier Laniel at Bermuda are printed in Hansard, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Parliamentary Debates,</hi> 5th Series, vol. 522, pp.
                    578–586.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d138-f3" n="3" xml:id="d138-f3-content">
                <p>Regarding Secretary Dulles’ comments at Paris in December 1953 regarding an
                    “agonizing reappraisal” of U.S. commitments to European defense should the EDC
                    treaty not be ratified, see the editorial note, <ref
                        target="frus1952-54v05p1#pg_868">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>,
                        Part 1, p. 868</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
